---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const description =
  'How the factory is put together: the engine, the command layer, and the operational subsystems: sandbox, healer, ticketing, registry.';
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Architecture"
  description={description}
  current="/docs/architecture"
  prev={{ href: '/docs/core-concepts', label: 'Core concepts' }}
  next={{ href: '/docs/configuration', label: 'Configuration' }}
>
  <p>
    The system is a thin dispatch layer over focused modules. The CLI entry point
    (<code class="hl">src/sssf/cli.py</code>) only parses arguments and delegates; the logic lives
    in <code class="hl">src/sssf/commands/</code>, the engine lives in
    <code class="hl">src/sssf/adw_modules/</code>, and the operational subsystems
    (sandbox, healer, ticketing, registry) sit beside them.
  </p>

  <pre><code>src/sssf/
├── cli.py                 # argparse dispatch only
├── registry.py            # ~/.sssf/projects.json: the global project registry
├── project.py             # find_project / data_dir resolution
├── obs.py                 # read-side queries over the WAL trace db
├── sandbox.py             # worktree + container lifecycle (spawn, monitor, teardown)
├── healer.py              # self-healing monitor daemon
├── ticketing.py           # ticket schema, providers, sync
├── adw_modules/           # the engine: everything ADWs import
│   ├── session.py         #   session lifecycle + tracer
│   ├── runner.py          #   phase execution, retries, gates
│   ├── agents.py          #   config load/validate, roster resolution
│   ├── agent_pi.py        #   the pi coding-agent adapter (tail JSONL -> events)
│   ├── agent_cc.py        #   claude_code adapter (schema-valid, stubbed in v1)
│   ├── data_types.py      #   ALL typed envelopes, params, config models
│   ├── gates.py           #   deterministic acceptance gates
│   ├── quality.py         #   known-command checks (lint / typecheck / build / test)
│   ├── git_helper.py      #   commits, diffs, baseline capture
│   ├── changes.py         #   change capture (what the working tree contains)
│   ├── permissions.py     #   post-call enforcement of writes: + protected_files
│   ├── prompts.py         #   prompt assembly from prompt_engineering/ templates
│   ├── tracer.py          #   event insertion into the trace db
│   ├── console.py         #   rich console rendering
│   └── utils.py
├── commands/              # one module per CLI group
│   ├── init.py  run.py  obs_cmds.py  viz.py  sweep.py
│   ├── sandbox_cmd.py  heal.py  ticket.py  misc.py
└── templates/             # EXACTLY what `sssf init` stamps into a project
    ├── adws/adw_*.py              # the starter chains
    ├── sssf.config.yaml           # the starter roster
    ├── prompt_engineering/&#123;agent&#125;/
    ├── harness_engineering/       # pi extensions
    └── env.sample</code></pre>

  <h2>The engine: adw_modules</h2>
  <p>
    ADW scripts stay thin on purpose: all low-level logic lives in
    <code class="hl">adw_modules/</code>. The most important pieces:
  </p>

  <h3>session.py: lifecycle + trace</h3>
  <p>
    <code class="hl">session.ensure(cfg, adw_id)</code> pins or creates the run session: same
    dirs, same <code class="hl">context_handoff/</code>, and each agent resumes its existing
    context window through <code class="hl">agent_map.json</code> instead of starting cold. That is
    how you chain workflows: supply the same <code class="hl">--adw-id</code> and the run joins
    that session.
  </p>

  <h3>runner.py: phases, retries, gates</h3>
  <p>
    The <code class="hl">run.phase(PhaseParams(...))</code> context manager executes the phase,
    flips its status, records it, and runs the declared gates. Retries re-prompt the same session
    with a correction; they never restart cold.
  </p>

  <h3>agents.py: the roster</h3>
  <p>
    Loads <code class="hl">sssf.config.yaml</code>, merges per-agent overrides over
    <code class="hl">defaults</code>, and validates that every name in
    <code class="hl">REQUIRED_AGENTS</code> exists and every model is written
    <code class="hl">provider/model-id</code> before anything spawns.
  </p>

  <h3>agent_pi.py: the pi adapter</h3>
  <p>
    The v1 coding agent is <span class="cmd">pi</span>. The adapter builds the exact pi invocation
    (mode, JSON output, provider, model, session id), spawns it, and tails its JSONL stdout line by
    line, folding each event into the trace while the agent is still working. Pi announces a tool
    call across three raw events; the interface folds them into exactly one
    <code class="hl">tool_call</code> row per real call.
  </p>

  <h3>permissions.py: writes is the boundary</h3>
  <p>
    After every agent call, the repo&rsquo;s state is compared against the agent&rsquo;s declared
    <code class="hl">writes:</code> and <code class="hl">defaults.protected_files</code>.
    Unauthorized changes are rolled back and the phase dies. A read-only agent is read-only with
    respect to the <em>repo</em>: the session runtime under <code class="hl">data_dir</code> is
    always writable, so it is never mute.
  </p>

  <h3>quality.py: known commands as code</h3>
  <p>
    Lint, typecheck, build, and test suites are invoked through
    <code class="hl">quality.py</code> as <code class="hl">kind="code"</code> phases. The shipped
    blocks are placeholders that exit 0; wiring your real commands here is the first thing to
    customize (see <a href="/sssf/docs/configuration">Configuration</a>).
  </p>

  <h3>git_helper.py + changes.py</h3>
  <p>
    Commits, diffs, and baseline capture. The commit phase is the whole pattern in miniature: the
    builder <em>proposes</em> a commit message as a field on its envelope, code decides whether to
    use it (falling back when empty) and performs the write. The agent never runs
    <code class="hl">git commit</code> itself.
  </p>

  <h2>The trace db</h2>
  <p>
    Seven tables, ten event types, WAL mode. The writer is the tracer
    (<code class="hl">adw_modules/tracer.py</code>); the reader is
    <code class="hl">obs.py</code> plus the visualizer&rsquo;s own queries. One cursor query
    (<code class="hl">where adw_id = ? and rowid &gt; ?</code>) is the entire transport: live view
    and history are the same query at different cadence.
  </p>

  <h2>Operational subsystems</h2>

  <h3>sandbox.py: parallel-safe runs</h3>
  <p>
    Each run executes in its own git worktree (branch <code class="hl">sssf/&lt;adw_id&gt;</code>)
    bind-mounted into a <code class="hl">sssf-runner</code> container. A monitor tears the sandbox
    down when the ADW exits (success or fail). Each run keeps its own per-run db; a forward-only
    sync streams progress into the project db without lock fights. See
    <a href="/sssf/docs/sandbox">Sandboxed runs</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>healer.py: the monitor daemon</h3>
  <p>
    Scans every registered project and recovers the stuck: dead sandboxes, torn terminal state,
    hung agents (no progress for 10 minutes), spawn-failed tickets, with a restart budget of 3
    per session. State lives in <code class="hl">~/.sssf/heal-state.json</code>. See
    <a href="/sssf/docs/healer">Healer</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>ticketing.py: the backlog</h3>
  <p>
    A <code class="hl">tickets</code> table in the project db, an internal provider, and a sync
    interface for external providers (per-project <code class="hl">ticketing.yaml</code>). Tickets
    flow Backlog → Planning → Building → Reviewing → Done/Blocked on the kanban. See
    <a href="/sssf/docs/ticketing">Ticketing</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>registry.py: one install, many projects</h3>
  <p>
    <code class="hl">~/.sssf/projects.json</code> maps project name → root → last run. Every
    registered project is a first-class citizen of <span class="cmd">sssf viz</span>, the sweep,
    and the healer.
  </p>

  <h2>The visualizer</h2>
  <p>
    A Vue + Vite app served by Bun (<code class="hl">src/sssf/apps/visualizer/</code>), polled
    against the trace db. It runs as a background service under <span class="cmd">sssf viz</span>
    with its state in <code class="hl">~/.sssf/</code> (<code class="hl">viz.pid</code>,
    <code class="hl">viz.log</code>). Every view is documented in
    <a href="/sssf/docs/visualizer">Visualizer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stamped vs. what is package code</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="cmd">sssf init</span> stamps only the <strong>customization surface</strong>:
    chains, roster, prompts, harness engineering. The engine is package code: changing it is
    contributing, not customizing. This boundary is what makes <span class="cmd">sssf upgrade</span>
    safe: your project&rsquo;s stamped files are yours, the engine moves forward underneath them.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
